1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,975
Every file on a computer is just numbers.

2
00:00:03,525 --> 00:00:06,277
Text, pictures, programs: all of it is bytes.

3
00:00:06,827 --> 00:00:09,621
And many server bugs come from mixing those bytes up.

4
00:00:10,171 --> 00:00:11,890
Today we look at real bytes,

5
00:00:11,890 --> 00:00:12,503
encodings,

6
00:00:12,503 --> 00:00:12,871
paths,

7
00:00:12,871 --> 00:00:14,467
and environment variables,

8
00:00:14,467 --> 00:00:15,633
live in a terminal.

9
00:00:17,683 --> 00:00:19,816
First, the smallest piece: the bit.

10
00:00:20,366 --> 00:00:22,798
A bit is one switch, either zero or one.

11
00:00:23,348 --> 00:00:24,842
Eight bits make one byte.

12
00:00:25,392 --> 00:00:28,707
One byte holds two hundred fifty-six different values,

13
00:00:28,707 --> 00:00:30,917
from zero to two hundred fifty-five.

14
00:00:31,467 --> 00:00:35,264
Files, memory, and network traffic are all counted in bytes.

15
00:00:37,314 --> 00:00:38,871
Let us look at a real byte.

16
00:00:39,421 --> 00:00:41,683
The tool xxd shows raw bytes.

17
00:00:42,233 --> 00:00:45,689
Type: echo -n A | xxd -b

18
00:00:45,819 --> 00:00:49,531
Eight bits: zero one zero zero zero zero zero one.

19
00:00:50,081 --> 00:00:54,091
That pattern is the number sixty-five, and sixty-five is the letter A.

20
00:00:54,641 --> 00:00:59,697
Now the same, without dash b: echo -n A | xxd

21
00:00:59,827 --> 00:01:03,027
Forty-one. That is sixty-five written in hexadecimal.

22
00:01:03,577 --> 00:01:04,377
Hexadecimal,

23
00:01:04,377 --> 00:01:04,843
or hex,

24
00:01:04,843 --> 00:01:07,576
writes one byte with just two characters.

25
00:01:07,576 --> 00:01:09,508
Developers use it everywhere.

26
00:01:10,058 --> 00:01:15,093
One more, with two letters. Type: echo -n Hi | xxd

27
00:01:15,223 --> 00:01:19,745
Hex forty-eight is H, and hex sixty-nine is i. Two letters, two bytes.

28
00:01:20,295 --> 00:01:22,962
That is all a text file is: bytes, in a row.

29
00:01:25,012 --> 00:01:28,319
But a byte is only a number. How does a number become a letter?

30
00:01:28,869 --> 00:01:32,453
By agreement. A table says which number means which character.

31
00:01:33,003 --> 00:01:37,376
The oldest common table is ASCII, with one hundred twenty-eight characters.

32
00:01:37,926 --> 00:01:43,857
In it, sixty-five is capital A, sixty-six is capital B, and ninety-seven is lowercase a.

33
00:01:44,407 --> 00:01:48,012
So a text file is simply bytes, plus a table to read them with.

34
00:01:48,562 --> 00:01:51,314
Without the right table, the bytes are just numbers.

35
00:01:52,864 --> 00:01:56,277
Is a file text, or a program? The file command tells you.

36
00:01:56,827 --> 00:02:03,270
First, make a small text file. Type: echo "hello" > hello.txt

37
00:02:03,400 --> 00:02:05,875
Now type: file hello.txt

38
00:02:06,005 --> 00:02:09,439
ASCII text. Just bytes that follow the ASCII table.

39
00:02:09,989 --> 00:02:22,320
Now a real program. Type: file -b /bin/ls | cut -d, -f1-2

40
00:02:22,450 --> 00:02:26,951
An ELF executable. That is the standard Linux program format.

41
00:02:27,501 --> 00:02:33,965
Last, look inside it. Type: head -c 16 /bin/ls | xxd

42
00:02:34,095 --> 00:02:40,175
The first bytes are seven f, then E, L, F. Every Linux program starts with that signature.

43
00:02:40,725 --> 00:02:44,629
A program and a text file are both bytes. Only the meaning differs.

44
00:02:46,679 --> 00:02:51,308
A table of one hundred twenty-eight characters cannot hold the world's languages.

45
00:02:51,858 --> 00:02:57,661
So Unicode gives every character its own number: letters, accents, symbols, even emoji.

46
00:02:58,211 --> 00:03:01,795
UTF-8 is the way those numbers are stored as bytes.

47
00:03:02,345 --> 00:03:04,232
English letters take one byte.

48
00:03:04,232 --> 00:03:06,875
An accented letter like e acute takes two.

49
00:03:06,875 --> 00:03:08,510
Others take three or four.

50
00:03:09,060 --> 00:03:13,306
UTF-8 is the default on almost every modern Linux server.

51
00:03:13,856 --> 00:03:18,421
Keep two words apart: a character is what you see, and a byte is what is stored.

52
00:03:20,471 --> 00:03:24,119
Let us prove it. First, the plain word cafe, with no accent.

53
00:03:24,669 --> 00:03:27,229
Type: printf 'cafe' | xxd

54
00:03:27,359 --> 00:03:28,831
Four letters, four bytes.

55
00:03:29,381 --> 00:03:34,928
Now with the accent. Type: printf 'café' | xxd

56
00:03:35,058 --> 00:03:38,663
Five bytes! The e acute became two bytes: c three, a nine.

57
00:03:39,213 --> 00:03:43,586
Count the bytes. Type: printf 'café' | wc -c

58
00:03:43,716 --> 00:03:47,898
Five bytes. Now count characters. Type: printf 'café' | wc -m

59
00:03:48,028 --> 00:03:50,929
Four characters, five bytes. They are different things.

60
00:03:51,479 --> 00:03:57,067
Now the classic bug: read these bytes with the wrong table.

61
00:03:57,067 --> 00:04:00,951
Type: echo café | iconv -f latin1 -t utf8

62
00:04:01,081 --> 00:04:05,049
Garbled! Instead of e acute, we see A tilde and a copyright sign.

63
00:04:05,599 --> 00:04:08,761
That is the broken text you may have seen on websites.

64
00:04:08,761 --> 00:04:09,932
The bytes were fine.

65
00:04:09,932 --> 00:04:11,103
The table was wrong.

66
00:04:11,653 --> 00:04:17,093
The fix is to use the matching encoding. In practice, use UTF-8 everywhere.

67
00:04:19,143 --> 00:04:21,831
Next: how a text file marks the end of a line.

68
00:04:22,381 --> 00:04:24,621
Linux uses one byte, called L F.

69
00:04:25,171 --> 00:04:27,923
Windows uses two bytes: C R, then L F.

70
00:04:28,473 --> 00:04:32,846
A file made on Windows can carry hidden C R bytes onto your Linux server.

71
00:04:33,396 --> 00:04:37,066
The result: scripts that refuse to run, with confusing errors.

72
00:04:39,116 --> 00:04:42,444
Let us create a Windows-style file, and see for ourselves.

73
00:04:42,994 --> 00:04:50,695
Type: printf 'one\r\ntwo\r\n' > win.txt

74
00:04:50,825 --> 00:04:53,321
Now type: file win.txt

75
00:04:53,451 --> 00:04:57,227
The file command spots them: C R L F line terminators.

76
00:04:57,777 --> 00:05:00,764
Type: cat -A win.txt

77
00:05:00,894 --> 00:05:02,953
Dash A shows hidden characters.

78
00:05:02,953 --> 00:05:04,680
Caret M is the hidden C R,

79
00:05:04,680 --> 00:05:07,337
and the dollar sign marks each line end.

80
00:05:07,887 --> 00:05:11,023
Now a script with the same problem. First, make it.

81
00:05:11,573 --> 00:05:18,143
Type: printf '#!/bin/bash\r\necho hi\r\n' > run.sh

82
00:05:18,273 --> 00:05:22,945
Make it executable. Type: chmod +x run.sh

83
00:05:23,075 --> 00:05:26,403
Now run it. Type: ./run.sh

84
00:05:26,533 --> 00:05:29,840
Required file not found. But the file is right there!

85
00:05:30,390 --> 00:05:35,574
The system looks for an interpreter named bash plus a hidden C R, and that does not exist.

86
00:05:36,124 --> 00:05:44,124
The fix: s e d removes the C R at the end of every line. Type: sed -i 's/\r$//' run.sh

87
00:05:44,254 --> 00:05:45,150
Run it again.

88
00:05:46,400 --> 00:05:50,773
It prints hi. Remember this one: it is a very common real-world error.

89
00:05:52,823 --> 00:05:55,490
Now paths: how you tell Linux where a file is.

90
00:05:56,040 --> 00:05:59,389
An absolute path starts with a slash, and works from anywhere.

91
00:05:59,939 --> 00:06:04,206
A relative path does not start with a slash. It starts from where you are now.

92
00:06:04,756 --> 00:06:06,329
Four shortcuts matter.

93
00:06:06,329 --> 00:06:07,545
Slash is the top.

94
00:06:07,545 --> 00:06:08,404
Dot is here.

95
00:06:08,404 --> 00:06:10,192
Dot dot is one folder up.

96
00:06:10,192 --> 00:06:12,052
Tilde is your home folder.

97
00:06:12,602 --> 00:06:16,698
Rule of thumb: in scripts and on servers, absolute paths are safer.

98
00:06:18,748 --> 00:06:25,553
Let us walk a path. First, make two folders at once. Type: mkdir -p project/docs

99
00:06:25,683 --> 00:06:30,419
Move in, using a relative path. Type: cd project/docs

100
00:06:30,549 --> 00:06:32,768
Where are we? Type: pwd

101
00:06:32,898 --> 00:06:34,925
It prints the full, absolute path.

102
00:06:35,475 --> 00:06:39,400
Now dot dot, one folder up. Type: cd ..

103
00:06:39,950 --> 00:06:42,169
Check again. Type: pwd

104
00:06:42,299 --> 00:06:46,565
Now tilde, which takes us home from anywhere. Type: cd ~

105
00:06:46,695 --> 00:06:50,066
List with a relative path. Type: ls project

106
00:06:50,196 --> 00:06:55,465
And with an absolute path. Type: ls /home/student/project

107
00:06:55,595 --> 00:06:59,393
Same folder, same answer. Choose by where the command will run.

108
00:07:01,443 --> 00:07:05,496
Now environment variables: named values that programs can read.

109
00:07:06,046 --> 00:07:10,910
Your shell has variables. When you export one, it becomes an environment variable.

110
00:07:11,460 --> 00:07:16,047
Child programs, such as scripts you start, inherit only the exported ones.

111
00:07:16,597 --> 00:07:19,157
Common ones are HOME, USER, SHELL, and PATH.

112
00:07:19,707 --> 00:07:23,526
Servers use them for settings, such as which database to talk to.

113
00:07:25,576 --> 00:07:27,112
Let us watch this happen.

114
00:07:27,662 --> 00:07:31,715
Type: echo $HOME. The dollar sign means: give me the value.

115
00:07:32,265 --> 00:07:38,431
Now make our own variable. Type: GREETING=hello. No spaces around the equals sign.

116
00:07:38,981 --> 00:07:41,733
Read it back. Type: echo $GREETING

117
00:07:41,863 --> 00:07:49,841
Now start a child shell, and ask it to print the variable.

118
00:07:50,391 --> 00:07:53,975
Empty! The child cannot see it, because we have not exported it.

119
00:07:54,525 --> 00:07:57,107
So export it. Type: export GREETING

120
00:07:57,237 --> 00:08:01,439
Press the up arrow twice, to repeat the child shell command, then Enter.

121
00:08:01,989 --> 00:08:06,811
Now the child sees hello. Exported variables flow down to child programs.

122
00:08:08,861 --> 00:08:12,744
One environment variable deserves special attention: PATH.

123
00:08:13,294 --> 00:08:16,366
PATH is a list of folders, separated by colons.

124
00:08:16,916 --> 00:08:21,204
When you type a command name, the shell searches those folders, from left to right.

125
00:08:21,754 --> 00:08:25,082
If the program is in none of them, you get: command not found.

126
00:08:25,632 --> 00:08:30,368
The fix: put the program in a folder on the path, or add its folder to the path.

127
00:08:32,418 --> 00:08:35,618
Let us see our own path. Type: echo $PATH

128
00:08:35,748 --> 00:08:37,881
Each folder is separated by a colon.

129
00:08:38,431 --> 00:08:41,525
Which command was found where? Type: which ls

130
00:08:41,655 --> 00:08:44,023
The which command prints the file that would run.

131
00:08:44,573 --> 00:08:50,717
Now our own script. First, a folder for it. Type: mkdir -p ~/bin

132
00:08:50,847 --> 00:08:57,183
Create a one-line script. Type: printf '#!/bin/bash\necho "Hello!"\n' > ~/bin/hello

133
00:08:57,313 --> 00:09:02,753
Make it executable. Type: chmod +x ~/bin/hello

134
00:09:02,883 --> 00:09:05,123
Now run it by name. Type: hello

135
00:09:05,253 --> 00:09:08,410
Command not found. The folder is not on the path yet.

136
00:09:08,960 --> 00:09:14,592
Add it. Type: export PATH="$HOME/bin:$PATH"

137
00:09:14,722 --> 00:09:16,621
Run it again. Type: hello

138
00:09:16,751 --> 00:09:20,292
It works! And which shows where it was found. Type: which hello

139
00:09:20,422 --> 00:09:23,061
This is how every installed command works.

140
00:09:23,061 --> 00:09:26,579
A change like this lasts only for this terminal session.

141
00:09:26,579 --> 00:09:29,595
Saving it permanently comes later in the course.

142
00:09:31,645 --> 00:09:34,546
Let us connect all this to the errors you will really meet.

143
00:09:35,096 --> 00:09:38,083
Command not found: check the path, and check the spelling.

144
00:09:38,633 --> 00:09:43,348
Strange characters like A tilde: an encoding mismatch. Use UTF-8.

145
00:09:43,898 --> 00:09:49,508
Required file not found, or caret M: hidden C R bytes. Convert the line endings.

146
00:09:50,058 --> 00:09:53,711
No such file or directory: usually a wrong path.

147
00:09:53,711 --> 00:09:56,069
Check relative versus absolute,

148
00:09:56,069 --> 00:09:56,906
with p w d.

149
00:09:57,456 --> 00:10:00,827
A variable is empty in a script: you forgot to export it.

150
00:10:02,877 --> 00:10:04,733
Watch out for four common myths.

151
00:10:05,283 --> 00:10:10,318
Myth one: all text files are the same. In fact, encoding and line endings differ.

152
00:10:10,868 --> 00:10:16,244
Myth two: a character is one byte. In fact, in UTF-8 it can take up to four.

153
00:10:16,794 --> 00:10:21,187
Myth three: a variable set in the terminal reaches every program.

154
00:10:21,187 --> 00:10:21,728
In fact,

155
00:10:21,728 --> 00:10:24,431
only exported ones reach child programs.

156
00:10:24,981 --> 00:10:28,935
Myth four: command not found means the program is not installed.

157
00:10:28,935 --> 00:10:29,430
In fact,

158
00:10:29,430 --> 00:10:31,530
it may simply be outside the path.

159
00:10:32,080 --> 00:10:33,424
Check before you assume.

160
00:10:35,474 --> 00:10:37,799
Here is how you check your own understanding.

161
00:10:38,349 --> 00:10:41,549
Imagine a colleague asks you to explain a broken script.

162
00:10:42,099 --> 00:10:44,617
Explain bits, bytes, and hex for one byte.

163
00:10:45,167 --> 00:10:48,431
Tell a text file from a program, using the file command.

164
00:10:48,981 --> 00:10:51,775
Show bytes versus characters in UTF-8.

165
00:10:52,325 --> 00:10:54,992
Find and remove hidden C R line endings.

166
00:10:55,542 --> 00:10:58,038
Use absolute and relative paths correctly.

167
00:10:58,588 --> 00:11:02,001
Export a variable, and fix a command that is not on the path.

168
00:11:02,551 --> 00:11:04,173
To pass, you need all six.

169
00:11:06,223 --> 00:11:09,124
Your turn, in the virtual machine you built last time.

170
00:11:09,674 --> 00:11:13,322
One: use x x d to show the bytes of your own first name.

171
00:11:13,872 --> 00:11:17,670
Two: pick a word with an accent. Show its bytes, then its characters.

172
00:11:18,220 --> 00:11:22,614
Three: make a file with C R L F endings, find them, and remove them.

173
00:11:23,164 --> 00:11:26,876
Four: export a variable, and prove that a child shell can see it.

174
00:11:27,426 --> 00:11:30,562
Five: put a script in your bin folder, and run it by name.

175
00:11:31,112 --> 00:11:34,419
Mark yourself on the rubric. Pause the video, and do it now.

176
00:11:49,969 --> 00:11:50,844
Quick check.

177
00:11:51,394 --> 00:11:53,783
How many bits make a byte, and what is hex?

178
00:11:54,333 --> 00:11:56,914
Why can a word have more bytes than characters?

179
00:11:57,464 --> 00:11:59,768
What does command not found usually mean?

180
00:12:00,318 --> 00:12:03,689
What is the difference between a variable and an exported variable?

181
00:12:04,239 --> 00:12:08,634
Objective covered: MC02-O06.

182
00:12:09,184 --> 00:12:11,253
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

183
00:12:21,803 --> 00:12:24,960
Next lesson: server components and failure symptoms.

184
00:12:25,510 --> 00:12:27,388
Subscribe, so you do not miss it.
